const unit1Deck = [

  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "What does IT project management include, what range of outcomes can IT projects have, and what skills does an IT PM need beyond knowledge, skills, tools and techniques?", "answer": "IT project management includes: 1) the development of plans, 2) the monitoring of work against these plans, 3) the organization of work, 4) accountability for achieving goals specific to the project. Outcomes range from a mobile app to a fully fledged system serving the needs of organizations of thousands of employees across the globe, all with constantly changing technology that may radically influence customers’ needs and consequently the project direction. Beyond knowledge, skills, tools and techniques, the PM needs domain expertise, must excel in ‘soft’ skills (leadership, communication, negotiation, teamwork, etc.), and must understand human behavior, culture, and work ethic."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "Define a project: etymology, the PMBOK definition, why ‘unique’ is emphasized, and the consequence of projects creating something new. Note the summary inconsistency. Include the tutor’s project characteristics and project sizes (tutor-only).", "answer": "Etymology: ‘project’ from Latin ‘proiectum’ = something thrown forward; ‘manage’ from ‘manus’ = hand, so project management is handling something that will materialize as we move forward. PMBOK (PMI’s core publication; Larson & Gray, 2015, p. 2): a project is ‘a temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique product, service, or result.’ ‘Unique’ distinguishes project management from operations, where activities like production are repeated (replicated in essence). Facing something new challenges our ability to respond based on experience, causing frustration and frequent failure in projects. INCONSISTENCY: the Unit 1 summary drops ‘result’ (‘unique product or service’); always use the full PMBOK definition. (tutor-only) Three characteristics: definite start and end; a unique outcome/output; it needs resources (human, equipment, materials). Brushing your teeth daily is NOT a project (repetitive routine, no unique deliverable, no start/end). (tutor-only) Size is defined by number of people, complexity, and duration: small = e.g. two programmers plus one PM/technical lead updating reports or features; mega = multi-year, e.g. an SAP–Oracle ERP integration after a merger running about five years."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "Why are airplanes mostly built on time, on budget and safely, while IT projects often fail? Include the two views on changing requirements and the glossary definition of Agile.", "answer": "Scientists and engineers have established working methodologies and practices that sufficiently address the needs of modern projects. Airplanes succeed because of the extreme detail of their design; when the design is frozen, the contractor has little flexibility to change specifications (the customer rarely requests a shorter wingspan halfway through construction). In IT, specifications are viewed as flexible and open to change until the end of the process: e.g. the customer requests that a menu option be added, a button be moved in the user interface, or additional functionality be considered. View 1: changing or unclear requirements are often considered the cause of the high failure rate of IT projects (Hussain & Mkpojiogu, 2016). View 2: changing requirements and a changing environment are facts of life that projects need to cope with, which led to ‘agile’ approaches that put particular emphasis on supporting changes even late in the project. Glossary – Agile: a method of project management mostly used in relation to software development."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "What two further challenges come from IT being a young field with hidden failures? Include the related Mastersolution key.", "answer": "1) The IT field is relatively new, at least compared to the decades of experience in other engineering disciplines, so the methods and practices applied need to be revisited throughout the execution of a project. 2) When a failure occurs in the IT industry (especially in the software domain), very little is revealed, and many times the failure is rationalized, covered up, or ignored, resulting in the repetition of mistakes by others or even by the same organization that developed the solution. Mastersolution practice exam: IT projects uniquely differ from engineering projects in risk assessment because ‘it is difficult to recognize from the outside when a failure is imminent’ (wrong options: they fail more frequently / requirements are more strict / they fail less frequently). The exact phrase is not in the coursebook; this hidden-failures passage is the basis."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "Whose needs must IT projects meet inside the organization? Describe Figure 1 (The Technology View of an Organization) level by level, and the figure vs text inconsistency.", "answer": "IT projects must meet the needs of various stakeholders within an organization as well as outside (Alreemy, Chang, Walters, & Wills, 2016). Figure 1 is a pyramid titled ‘Stakeholders needs – IT projects’, top to bottom: 1) Shareholder level: top-level needs and interests. 2) Executive level: strategic information that will guide the future direction of the organization. 3) Management level (middle management): information needs to be converted into meaningful patterns and knowledge to assist in decision-making. 4) Operational level: information needs of day-to-day business operations; specialized IT systems for finance and accounting, sales and marketing, human resources, and manufacturing and production. INCONSISTENCY: the text names only three levels (operational, middle management, executives); the shareholder level is figure-only."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "Which external parties influence IT projects, how does the coursebook describe them, and what does each need from the organization’s information systems?", "answer": "Organizations do not operate in isolation, so a multitude of external factors and stakeholders influence the design and behavior of IT projects, in addition to the internal organizational needs (de Oliveira & Rabechini Jr., 2019). Customers, suppliers, collaborators, and governments form the mix of ‘willing and unwilling partners’ that impose on an organization’s information systems and in many cases critically influence how these are built and operated. Customers: access to online services such as searching for product and service information, purchasing, and inquiry submission. Suppliers and business partners: access to the organization’s databases for stock estimates, delivery schedules, and financial transactions. Governments and regulatory bodies: access to compliance information and operational details, to ensure the consumer and the state are protected from unlawful behavior and liabilities and that the organization functions and behaves in socially conscious ways."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "What is IT infrastructure composed of, and why is IT unlike any other discipline? Explain the bridge vs tablet example, the human element, and sociotechnical systems.", "answer": "IT infrastructure is typically composed of hardware and software, which are tightly interdependent, a peculiarity of IT that makes it unlike any other discipline. Bridge vs tablet: a bridge meets its purpose once built; it needs no software, and people and vehicles cross it without support from outside entities. A tablet cannot complete its purpose without software to make things happen. IT systems also interface with humans at a cognitive level, so an operator is usually required somewhere in the system (e.g. a user surfing the internet, a data entry person). This interconnection of humans and technology classifies IT systems as sociotechnical systems (Teubner, 2018). The human element is frequently so critical that it is impossible to design a system that satisfies the variable operational needs of different entities, resulting in customizations and product versions that can only be revealed after the release of the product or service."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "How is project success defined, and why is it complex? Explain the iron triangle (text, glossary, Figure 2), the definition of scope, and how changing one constraint affects the others.", "answer": "Success is often defined as meeting the objectives established at the beginning (Davis, 2017). It is complex because the product or service is rarely defined exactly, and other parameters like time and cost might be involved (Neves, Borgman, & Heier, 2016). This multiplicity of objectives is depicted in the iron triangle: a simplified concept depicting the interdependence of project scope, schedule, and cost (Pollack, Helm, & Adler, 2018; Sanchez & Terlizzi, 2017). Project constituents must balance the three to ensure the project objectives and goals (as proxies to project success) are met. Glossary: also known as the project management triangle; time, scope, and cost together contribute to the quality of a project, with changes to one element affecting the other two (time = schedule). Figure 2: a triangle labelled ‘Triple constraints’, with Schedule at the top and Scope and Cost at the bottom corners. Scope = the boundaries of the project concerning what is included and excluded. Interdependence: widening (adding features) or narrowing (removing features) the scope can trigger schedule changes, which influence cost as resources are added or removed; a change to budget or schedule affects the realizable scope. Example 1: shortening the schedule to accelerate delivery engages more resources, so cost increases. Example 2: widening the scope (adding capabilities to an application) stretches the schedule or needs more developers (higher cost). Side effect of any change to the constraints: the risk of things going wrong."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "Why might a quintuple constraints view be adopted? Name and define the five constraints, including what Figure 3 lists under each.", "answer": "Given the complexity of IT projects, three constraints are often not enough, so a quintuple (or even higher) constraints view might be adopted (Gransberg & Jeong, n.d.). The constraints are grouped to display their interdependency and the balancing act required to keep them within approved ranges. 1) Schedule (Figure 3: time, schedule risk, prescribed milestones, availability of resources). 2) Cost (Figure 3: project estimates, uncertainty, contingency, project-related costs, project cost drivers and constraints). 3) Context: the view of the environment in which the project’s product or service will operate, from local or global concerns (including cultural and customization aspects) to the political and social environments the project and its outcomes are exposed to (Figure 3: stakeholders, local issues, environmental, legal/legislative, global/national, unexpected occurrences, project-specific issues). 4) Technical: inherent to IT projects, imposed by technology choices at the hardware, communications, and software levels; essentially corresponds to the scope constraint of the iron triangle (Figure 3: scope of work, internal structure, contract, design, construction, technology, nature of constraints). 5) Financing: may be restricted, e.g. when multiple private and government stakeholders are involved and when operations span time and geographical locations (Figure 3: public financing, financing a future revenue stream, exploiting asset value, finance-driven project delivery methods, financial techniques to mitigate risk, differential inflation rates, commodity-based estimating). Figure 3 shows the five around a pentagon labelled ‘Quintuple constraints’."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "List and explain the six differences between IT projects and traditional engineering projects (Hidding & Nicholas, 2017).", "answer": "1) Supporting role: IT projects usually support other organizational functions (communications, payroll, accounting, etc.), so in addition to technical complexity they must address political, social, and other business factors. 2) Carriers of change: IT systems require widespread collaboration among business units and have a direct impact on organizational culture and structure. 3) Unclear scope: their widespread use means the boundaries are not always clear, which frequently results in customizations that impact the complexity of their interfaces with humans and machines. 4) Old and new: they often need to accommodate both, and replacing legacy systems is costly. 5) Tentative status: constant changes and advancements in technology mean IT systems always need to be updated with the latest version to stay current. 6) Management expectations: the fast pace of technological achievement leads management to expect better and more efficient delivery of services to keep up with technological growth and keep the organization competitive."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "MCQ (IU unit test): How do IT projects differ from traditional engineering projects?\nA) IT projects must comply with strict government regulations.\nB) IT projects target mostly internal stakeholders in the organization.\nC) IT projects do not have clear objectives and outcomes.\nD) IT projects cost more.", "answer": "B (confirmed IU key). It is based on IT projects serving a supporting role to other organizational functions. INCONSISTENCY: the coursebook also says IT projects must meet the needs of stakeholders within an organization as well as outside. In the MCQ, choose B; in an essay, mention both."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "MCQ (IU unit test): Which of the following is not part of the ‘iron triangle’ constraints?\nA) scope\nB) context\nC) cost\nD) schedule", "answer": "B. Context belongs to the quintuple constraints (schedule, cost, context, technical, financing). The iron triangle is scope, schedule, and cost."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "MCQ: Which quintuple constraint essentially corresponds to the scope constraint of the iron triangle?\nA) Context\nB) Financing\nC) Technical\nD) Schedule", "answer": "C. Technical constraints, imposed by technology choices at the hardware, communications, and software levels."},
  {"tag": "1.1", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain what an IT project is and discuss what makes IT projects and their management challenging compared with traditional engineering projects. Refer to the stakeholders involved and the constraints that must be balanced.", "answer": "Definition and management: IT project management includes the development of plans, monitoring of work against these plans, organization of work, and accountability for achieving project goals. Projects range from a mobile app to fully fledged global systems, under constantly changing technology that may radically influence customers’ needs and the project direction; PMs need domain expertise, soft skills (leadership, communication, negotiation, teamwork), and an understanding of human behavior, culture, and work ethic. ‘Project’ comes from proiectum (thrown forward), ‘manage’ from manus (hand). PMBOK: ‘a temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique product, service, or result’ (Larson & Gray, 2015); uniqueness distinguishes projects from repeated operations, and facing something new causes frustration and frequent failure.\n\nVersus engineering: airplanes succeed through the extreme detail of a frozen design; IT specifications stay flexible until the end (menu options, moved buttons, extra functionality). Changing or unclear requirements are often blamed for IT’s high failure rate; others see them as facts of life, which led to agile approaches that support late changes. The field is relatively new, so methods are revisited during execution, and failures are rationalized, covered up, or ignored, so mistakes repeat (failure is hard to recognize from the outside). Hardware and software are tightly interdependent, unlike any other discipline (bridge vs tablet). Systems interface with humans at a cognitive level via an operator, making them sociotechnical systems; variable needs cause customizations and versions revealed after release. Hidding & Nicholas (2017): supporting role (political, social, business factors); carriers of change (culture and structure); unclear scope boundaries (customizations, complex human and machine interfaces); old and new (costly legacy replacement); tentative status (constant updates); management expects better, more efficient delivery.\n\nStakeholders: within and outside the organization. Figure 1: shareholder (top-level needs and interests), executive (strategic information), management (meaningful patterns and knowledge for decisions), operational (day-to-day: finance and accounting, sales and marketing, HR, manufacturing and production). Externally, customers, suppliers, collaborators, and governments are willing and unwilling partners (online services; database access for stock, deliveries, finances; compliance information).\n\nConstraints: success = meeting the objectives set at the beginning, complicated by an inexactly defined product, time, and cost. Iron triangle (scope, schedule, cost) must be balanced: a shorter schedule raises cost; a wider scope stretches the schedule or raises cost; every change risks things going wrong. Complex IT projects use the quintuple view, adding context (local/global, cultural, customization, political and social), technical (hardware, communications, software choices; corresponds to scope), and financing (restricted with multiple private and government stakeholders or work spanning time and locations)."},

  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What range of work do IT projects cover, what do project teams need to succeed, and what does the coursebook say about organizational hierarchy and role variation?", "answer": "IT projects vary in size and type, from small software teams that develop a simple app to cross-functional teams that develop systems software and network infrastructures. To succeed, project teams need to be adequately resourced with appropriate staff and technology. As an organized activity, in addition to the leader/project manager, a more or less complex organizational hierarchy is typically adopted. Team structures vary, and roles might exist in isolation or be merged depending on the size of projects."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "Describe Figure 4 (Project Structure for Large Plan-Driven Projects) from top to bottom.", "answer": "Top: Project sponsor / PMO (one box). Below: Project manager, linked by a dashed two-way arrow to the Project management board (PMB). Below: Technical coordinator, linked by a dashed two-way arrow to the Technical management board (TMB). Below: five work-package leaders (WP1–WP5 leader). WP2 has three task leaders (Task 2.1, 2.2, 2.3) and WP5 has two (Task 5.1, 5.2); WP1, WP3 and WP4 have no task leaders, so their team members report directly to the WP leader. Bottom: team members. Arrows point upward (reporting lines). Task leaders exist only for complex WPs with multiple functionalities."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What is the role of the project sponsor and the PMO at the top of the project hierarchy, and why is this level crucial? Include the tutor’s view of the sponsor (tutor-only).", "answer": "The project sponsor is at the top and is responsible for the project’s business case. Large organizations often have a project management office (PMO), typically a separate entity in charge of major organizational projects (Monteiro, 2017); in 1.2 the book says PMOs are for sourcing project teams in terms of both labor and material support. This top level is crucial because it can make or break a project through its members’ extent of commitment and support. Their primary role is to ensure the project needs are addressed so the project delivers on its promises. (tutor-only) The sponsor is the one who wants the project, finances it and wants the result; typically top management of the company or the customer’s company. The PM reports to the sponsor."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What are the responsibilities of the project manager (PM) in a large project? Give both the management and the administrative/financial tasks.", "answer": "The PM is of critical importance to project success: in charge of the project and responsible for overall project management, including reporting to the project sponsor or PMO. Management tasks: interacting with the sponsor on contract-related issues, chairing regular management meetings, coordinating activities across the project, monitoring overall project performance, coordinating technical work, supervising the preparation of final deliverables, coordinating the use of the project’s results (Chen, Lee, & Chou, 2016). Administrative and financial tasks: representing the project in contract negotiations and in relation to the PMO, describing the project in workshops and official meetings, collecting administrative reports from project partners and forwarding periodical reports to the PMO or sponsor, preparing and updating the consortium agreement between participants, administering project resources and project spending, managing overall ethical and gender issues."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "How are smaller projects organized differently from large ones, and what are the supporting roles in Scrum? Include the glossary sidebars and their inconsistency.", "answer": "Smaller projects need less structure and planning and often put more emphasis on agile adaptation to changing requirements and environments. They often, but not always, have a PM or project leader but rarely a PMO. Instead of a PM, they sometimes stress self-organization of the development team, without any specific leadership role, but with supporting roles in Scrum: the product owner (managing and prioritizing the work to be done, focusing on optimizing the value to be gained) and the Scrum master (supporting the project processes and their implementation). Glossary – Scrum: ‘the practice of daily communication to enable reassessment of short-term working plans and goals in Agile Project Management situations.’ Glossary – Scrum Master: ‘responsible for the end product, as well as the monitoring of the production process and conduction of meetings – they are essentially the team leader.’ INCONSISTENCY: the sidebar calls the Scrum master ‘essentially the team leader’, while the text says there is no specific leadership role and the Scrum master only supports. Exam: use the text (supporting role); know the sidebar wording exists."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "Why is a technical coordinator (TC) created, and what are their responsibilities? What is the technical library?", "answer": "Due to the diverse specializations within IT, PMs often do not have all the background knowledge and skills to judge and oversee every technology involved, particularly in large or very specific projects. So a separate leadership role supporting the PM is created to address the technology needs: the technical coordinator. Responsibilities (Bogojević, 2017): monitoring all development plans and specifications; oversight of work-package leaders (WPL); establishing responsibilities and duties; establishing and maintaining the project’s technical library. The technical library is a historical account of what happened in the project: issues and their resolutions, plan alterations and their justifications, and anything of importance from a technical viewpoint. In large plan-driven projects the TC’s planning and distribution of work includes identifying the distinct work-packages (WP) that together constitute all project activity."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What are the responsibilities of work-package leaders (WPL), task leaders (TL) and team members?", "answer": "WPL: one is typically assigned per WP and is responsible for all actions in their WP, following the activities and objectives in the project plans, creating the respective deliverables on time and ensuring no delays occur. WPLs coordinate activities within the WP, work in close cooperation with the technical coordinator, and regularly (typically at least every few months and at milestones) submit progress reports to the TC or PM. TL: for complex WPs with multiple functionalities, tasks are broken down further and each gets its own task leader, with responsibilities similar to WPLs but focused on the specific task. Team members: at the bottom of the hierarchy; they perform the technical work and meet the specialized needs of the project."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What is resource pooling of team members, and what are its advantages and disadvantages? What do agile approaches prefer?", "answer": "While every other role in the hierarchy is traditionally distinct, team members may be shared among task teams depending on their specialization, forming a common resource pool from which projects satisfy their human labor needs. Advantages: may save money and support better use of the organization’s human resources. Disadvantages: loss of efficiency due to repeated context switches; loss of motivation if members do not feel they ‘belong’ to any one team; staffing issues if PMs exert political pressure and compete for resources. Many people therefore argue against resource pooling, and agile approaches in particular often emphasize assigning team members to only one team at a time."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What is the project management board (PMB): when is it used, what are its responsibilities, how often does it meet, who is in it, and what is its advisory role?", "answer": "In very large and complex IT projects, organizational charts are sometimes complemented with a PMB and/or TMB. The PMB, together with the project sponsor and/or PMO, is responsible for monitoring the overall progress and direction of the project, risk evaluation, resources used and costs incurred. It identifies reasons for deviations from the project plan and agrees on the necessary corrective actions. It resolves any differences between participants other than those of a technical nature. Major changes in the plan, such as reallocation of resources, may be decided by the PMB (within the limits of the agreements), as put forward by the sponsor or PMO. It convenes at regular intervals, at least every six months, to respond quickly and efficiently to issues. Representatives from all partners and project leadership are included, and the PM chairs the meetings. It also plays the role of an advisory board, a resource for the PM when feedback is needed; then it might include advisory members such as government representatives, policy makers, and leaders of local communities directly affected and involved."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What is the technical management board (TMB) and what are its responsibilities? How does it differ from the PMB?", "answer": "Especially in large IT projects, a TMB may be established to supplement the functionality of the PMB when technology aspects are addressed. It works to achieve the objectives set for the technical realization of the project by making appropriate decisions regarding work and resources. It often has decision-making responsibility for issues not solved by technical coordinators and WPLs (the book’s typo says ‘WLs’). It resolves technical aspects of the job shared by different contractors, coordinates contractual aspects, provides resolutions for technical conflicts, and audits and reviews activities in terms of time schedule and achievement against the planned milestones. Difference: the PMB handles management and non-technical issues (progress, risk, resources, costs, non-technical differences); the TMB handles technical issues and conflicts."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "How are project activities grouped in plan-driven vs agile development, and how does the coursebook define project phases?", "answer": "After establishing the organizational structure, the next step is identifying the activities to achieve the project goal and objectives. Plan-driven: activities are grouped into phases of logically related activities or stages of activities performed within the same period of time (Kneuper, 2018, p. 25). Agile: iterations, where a complete sequence of activities is performed repeatedly, e.g. analysis, design, implementation and possibly delivery in small circles once for each functionality, rather than a complete analysis for all functions followed by a complete design. Project phases are the steps in time required to build the final product, service, or solution (Larson & Gray, 2015). They are specific to the need the project addresses, are formed by logically collecting related activities that culminate in one or more deliverables, emphasis on certain phases is outcome-specific, and phases often (especially in IT) overlap to a great extent. General stages of IT projects are similar to other projects, but differ in how phases interrelate and in the resources involved."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "Describe Figure 5 (Generic Project Phases).", "answer": "A client/customer/user request feeds into five phases in sequence: concept/initiation → definition/planning → launch/execution → monitor/control → termination, ending in the product/service. A line beneath connects back up to all five phases. Slide note: the monitoring phase runs in parallel during the project; deviations of scope and schedule are evaluated and control is exerted if necessary to ensure expectations and plans. The unit summary names the same five phases and calls this an example of a plan-driven approach. 1.2 itself does not describe the termination phase in detail."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What triggers the concept/initiation phase and what happens in it? Include the feasibility study, PMO role and kick-off meeting.", "answer": "The need for a project is typically in response to a client/customer or user need; this triggers the conception or initiation phase, where all preliminary requirements are identified and the scope of the project is formulated. In this phase or prior to it, a feasibility study might be considered to establish the domain of the project and its potential for success given the available organizational resources and market demand. Regardless of a feasibility study, initiation involves assigning a project sponsor when possible and identifying and profiling the project stakeholders. Domain specifics are identified, including resources (human, equipment, facilities, etc.) and stakeholder expectations. When a PMO exists, its consulting with the team ensures procedures are established, policies are followed, and existing templates are adopted when necessary. A kick-off meeting is traditionally scheduled during initiation to bring the project constituents together and formalize the project’s commencement; workload assignment and preliminary planning could take place there."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What is a project charter, what does it contain, and what does signing it mean? Include the definition inconsistencies and the tutor’s view.", "answer": "A project charter is usually the outcome of the initiation phase and serves as a written acknowledgement that the project exists. Contents (Martin & Haggerty, 2017): the purpose or justification for the project; measurable objectives; a high-level description with its requirements and risks; an estimated milestone schedule and budget; criteria for project approval; the names of the PM and project sponsor with their respective authority levels. Signing the charter signifies the official authorization of the project. INCONSISTENCY: the glossary (Unit 6) defines it as ‘a statement of objectives that defines project goals as well as the roles and responsibilities of team members’; the slide calls it ‘a written acknowledgement of the project justification’. Exam default: ‘outcome of the initiation phase’ + ‘written acknowledgement that the project exists’. (tutor-only) It is an official document, not an agreement; it is issued at the start of the project, typically in the kick-off meeting, signed by the project sponsor and given to the PM to start the project."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "MCQ (IU unit test): A project charter is…\nA) the outcome of the feasibility study.\nB) developed by organizational leadership.\nC) an outcome of the initiation phase.\nD) a legally binding agreement.", "answer": "C. A charter is usually the outcome of the initiation phase. A feasibility study is separate (done in initiation or before it); the book never says leadership develops it; it is a written acknowledgement, not a legally binding agreement (legally binding contracts belong to procurement)."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What happens in the definition/planning phase? Include the baseline, subsidiary plans, additional activities, and the output.", "answer": "After initiation, project leadership must decide how to proceed, documented in some form of plan: an abstract or outlined conceptual view of coordinated actions defining how activities will be organized. A baseline is established for the project’s scope, schedule and cost, serving as the departure point for further elaboration and as the benchmark against which progress will be evaluated during execution (Sharma, Sohl, Hertogh, & Deketh, 2017). Subsidiary plans are developed for the project components and constraints and for quality and risk management, resources and procurement management, and stakeholder and communication management. Additional activities: selecting the life cycle of each phase, describing the tools and techniques to be used, the overlap and interactivity of processes, and how work will be executed. Output: the project plan, typically including dedicated plans for schedule, scope, risk, process improvement, human resources, procurement, configuration, communication and change management, plus plans for updating the scope, schedule and cost baselines."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "Explain iterations in real-world projects and the difference between classical (plan-driven) and agile project management.", "answer": "The book’s simple example includes no iterations, but in real-world projects iterations or loops are very important: one steps back to earlier phases to adapt existing results to a changing environment or new ideas from the customer. Classical project management usually works with fairly detailed plans and tries to cover the entire project from the start; because plans are so important, it is called ‘plan-driven’ project management. Agile project management emphasizes agility or flexibility and starts with a very superficial plan, with more detail added later, e.g. in sprint (iteration) planning in the Scrum methodology."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "What happens in the launch/execution phase and the monitor/control phase, and how do real projects deviate from the generic phases?", "answer": "Launch/execution: with leadership having approved the plan, development of the actual product or service commences. The ‘physical’ launch starts realizing the project components per the plan. The project team is acquired, and supporting infrastructure is procured or assigned. Quality assurance ensures the work meets anticipated requirements and standards (Owens & Khazanchi, 2017). Also: communication and stakeholder engagement, and review of deliverables as they are completed. Monitor/control: monitoring evaluates whether work is performed as planned, so it runs in parallel with most other planned work and is not just a short stage. Deviations of scope and schedule are evaluated and control is exerted to bring the project in line with stakeholder expectations and plans. If outcomes are not met, additional work may be required; in highly structured projects going back may not be possible, resulting in a failed project, at least in terms of its intended objectives. Deviations: always observed, especially in IT where complexity and requirements can be volatile or unclear. The sequence and iterations are project- and organization-specific; phases may overlap and run in parallel when the task breakdown allows, or iterations may be used, to maximize efficient use of resources and minimize time to delivery."},
  {"tag": "1.2", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Describe the typical roles in a large plan-driven IT project and explain the generic phases of an IT project, including the outcome of each phase where stated.", "answer": "Roles: teams need appropriate staff and technology and adopt a more or less complex hierarchy; roles vary with size and may be merged. Project sponsor (top, responsible for the business case) and PMO (separate entity in charge of major projects; sources teams with labor and material support); this level can make or break the project through commitment and support. Project manager: in charge, responsible for overall project management, reports to sponsor/PMO; chairs management meetings, coordinates activities and technical work, monitors performance, supervises final deliverables, coordinates use of results; administrative and financial tasks (contract negotiations, periodical reports, consortium agreement, resources and spending, ethical and gender issues). Technical coordinator: supports the PM on technology; monitors development plans and specifications, oversees WPLs, establishes responsibilities and duties, maintains the technical library (historical account of issues, resolutions, plan changes); identifies the work packages. WP leaders: responsible for all actions in their WP, deliverables on time, close cooperation with the TC, progress reports to TC/PM at least every few months and at milestones. Task leaders for complex WPs; team members perform the technical work. Team members may be pooled (saves money, better HR use) but this risks context switches, loss of belonging and political competition; agile prefers one team per member. Very large projects add a PMB (progress, direction, risk, resources, costs, corrective actions, non-technical disputes, major plan changes; meets at least every six months; PM chairs; also advisory with government, policy makers, local community leaders) and a TMB (technical decisions, unresolved TC/WPL issues, shared technical aspects between contractors, contractual coordination, technical conflicts, audits against milestones). Small projects: less structure, rarely a PMO, sometimes self-organization with a product owner and Scrum master in Scrum.\n\nPhases (Figure 5): plan-driven groups activities into phases (steps in time culminating in deliverables, often overlapping in IT); agile uses iterations. 1) Concept/initiation: triggered by a client/customer/user need; preliminary requirements and scope; optional feasibility study (domain and potential for success given resources and market demand); sponsor assigned; stakeholders identified and profiled; resources and expectations identified; PMO ensures procedures, policies and templates; kick-off meeting formalizes commencement. Outcome: the project charter, a written acknowledgement that the project exists (justification, measurable objectives, high-level description with requirements and risks, milestone schedule and budget, approval criteria, names of PM and sponsor with authority levels); signing it officially authorizes the project. 2) Definition/planning: plan; baseline for scope, schedule and cost as departure point and benchmark; subsidiary plans (quality, risk, resources, procurement, stakeholder, communication); life cycle, tools and techniques, how work is executed. Outcome: the project plan (schedule, scope, risk, process improvement, HR, procurement, configuration, communication, change management, baseline updates). Plan-driven = detailed plans from the start; agile = superficial plan detailed later in sprint planning; real projects iterate back to earlier phases. 3) Launch/execution: team acquired, infrastructure procured, quality assurance, stakeholder engagement, deliverable reviews. 4) Monitor/control: runs in parallel; deviations of scope and schedule evaluated and controlled; in highly structured projects failure to meet objectives may mean a failed project. 5) Termination, producing the product/service. In practice, deviations, overlaps and iterations are project- and organization-specific to maximize resource use and minimize time to delivery."},
  
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "What is project risk (coursebook definition), what are its two types, and why are positive risks still considered risks? Give the examples. Note the slide error.", "answer": "When decisions are made, there is always an associated risk that intended actions won’t produce the hoped-for results. Project risk refers to uncertainties in events or conditions that can influence the project outcome negatively or positively (Cagliano, Grimaldi, & Rafele, 2015). Negative risks = threats; positive risks = opportunities. Opportunities might seem welcome, but they are risks in terms of their impacts on the project constraints that were not initially anticipated, e.g. resources released from other projects becoming available ahead of schedule. Sometimes we even manufacture opportunities within the limits of risk tolerance, e.g. pushing the work and finishing ahead of time; this generates enhanced value, like rewards for the early release of project resources. SLIDE ERROR: Session 1 slide says ‘positively or negatively (known as threats)’; only negative risks are threats."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "Give the PMBOK definition of risk, its key property, the two attributes on which risks are evaluated, and how risks change over time.", "answer": "PMBOK: risk is an ‘uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, has a positive or negative effect on one or more project objectives’ (Larson & Gray, 2015, p. 309). Key property: risk is uncertain; we do not know whether or not it will occur. Risks and opportunities are evaluated on two attributes: 1) the probability that the risk will occur, 2) the size or amount of damage (or benefit) that might occur. At least the probability usually changes over time as more knowledge becomes available, and in most cases risks eventually cease to exist as risks after they either occur or can no longer occur, e.g. when the relevant project has finished."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "MCQ: On which two attributes are risks and opportunities usually evaluated?\nA) Cost and schedule\nB) Probability of occurrence and size of damage (or benefit)\nC) Likelihood and stakeholder interest\nD) Urgency and importance", "answer": "B. The probability that the risk will occur and the size or amount of damage (or benefit) that might occur."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "Name the general sources of risk in projects, why identifying them matters, and how known and unknown risks are handled.", "answer": "Sources of risk exist in almost every aspect of a project and its environment: inappropriate or inadequate staffing and/or technological and logistical support, limited funding, immature practices, lack of communication among the various stakeholders, dependencies on external participants outside the project’s direct control, etc. The ability to identify sources of risk and plan appropriate responses is of the utmost importance because things will go wrong at some point (Elzamly & Hussin, 2016). Known risks that cannot be managed proactively need contingency plans; management reserves must be planned to address unknown risks. Meeting schedule deadlines earlier than planned is one way to ensure there is enough time to deal with unknown threats that could cause delays."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "What are the three sources of risk specific to IT, and what examples does the coursebook give?", "answer": "Technological, organizational, and user-dependent (Taherdoost & Keshavarzsaleh, 2018). Technological: the technology of the product or service might not integrate or interface well with existing systems or the platforms it should support; the number of users might be underestimated; the system could lack the capability to scale up with demand. Organizational: lack of strategy for technology acquisition resulting in insufficient resourcing; underestimated staffing support requirements; many technology projects are requested by leadership or business units with no real understanding of what it takes to build an IT solution, which puts IT departments in a reactive mode with little room for influencing requirements. User-dependent: named as a source, but the coursebook gives no example."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "MCQ: Which is NOT one of the three sources of IT risk named in the coursebook?\nA) Technological\nB) Organizational\nC) User-dependent\nD) Environmental", "answer": "D. The three sources are technological, organizational, and user-dependent (Taherdoost & Keshavarzsaleh, 2018)."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "What determines whether a risk is acceptable? Explain risk attitude, the risk threshold, and Figure 6 (Risk Attitude). Include the tutor’s definition of risk appetite (tutor-only).", "answer": "Depending on the organization and its stakeholders, risk can be acceptable to varying degrees. The attitude toward risk (risk appetite and risk tolerance) is fundamentally what classifies a risk as acceptable or not; it can be influenced by environmental and idiosyncratic factors of the individual decision-maker and the organization. Risk-neutral actors are more reserved in their consideration of utilities (a proxy for uncertainty) and estimation and assignment of payoff (a proxy for gains), while risk-averse and risk-taking actors are more pessimistic or optimistic respectively. Risk threshold: the level of uncertainty or level of impact an individual or organization can afford toward a specific interest (Khan, Zhao, Kumar, & Stylianou, 2015); anything below it is acceptable, anything above cannot be tolerated. Figure 6: three graphs of utility (y) against payoff (x): risk-averse = concave curve rising fast then flattening; risk-neutral = straight line; risk-taking = convex curve starting flat then rising steeply. (tutor-only) Risk appetite/tolerance = how much risk an individual, project team or company can undertake; for full points add the three attitudes (risk-averse, risk-neutral, risk-taking)."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "Which factors influence an individual’s attitude toward risk, and what can they lead to?", "answer": "The individual’s perception of the situation, tolerances, biases, drives, interests, cognitive capabilities, and emotional state. These often lead to deliberate ignorance that influences the choice between which information is of value and which is irrelevant and excluded (Kutsch & Hall, 2010). Such ignorance is usually driven by social factors and/or conditioning, and is expressed as an affective impulse beyond one’s control, both systematically and cognitively."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "Describe the four-step process of managing risks.", "answer": "1) Identification: the risk must be identified as the basis for any further risk handling; an important step in project planning as well as project monitoring. 2) Analysis: clarify the risk’s probability, the size of the potential damage, and the measures that might be taken to address it. 3) Decision: based on the analysis, decide how to handle the risk (one of the four approaches). 4) Monitoring: check that the decision is implemented adequately and the risk stays at an acceptable level. While a project is running, risks considered very low commonly turn out more serious, e.g. several developers falling ill at the same time."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "Name and explain the four approaches to managing a risk, with the coursebook’s examples, and the factors that decide which one is applied.", "answer": "1) Risk mitigation: reduce the risk to an acceptable level by reducing the probability of it occurring or reducing the damage it could have should it occur. 2) Risk acceptance: applies when the risk is reasonably low; one accepts it without doing anything, possibly at least monitoring it. IT projects usually have a huge number of (very) small risks, and addressing all of them is not feasible. 3) Risk transfer: transfer the risk involved in an activity to an external entity, e.g. an insurance or a service provider. 4) Risk avoidance: applied if a risk is considered too high compared with the benefit involved, e.g. if the security risks of outsourcing a task are too high, the risk is avoided by not outsourcing it. The choice depends on the risk attitude, the cost of any mitigation or transfer activities, and the probability and potential damage involved. (tutor-only) Mitigation is used when a risk cannot be avoided and cannot simply be accepted because the damage would be too high."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "MCQ: A company decides not to outsource a task because the security risks are too high compared with the benefit. Which risk approach is this?\nA) Risk mitigation\nB) Risk transfer\nC) Risk acceptance\nD) Risk avoidance", "answer": "D. Risk avoidance: the risk is too high compared with the benefit, so the activity is not carried out. This is the coursebook’s own example."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "MCQ: Which risk approach is typically used for the huge number of very small risks in IT projects?\nA) Risk acceptance\nB) Risk transfer\nC) Risk avoidance\nD) Risk mitigation", "answer": "A. Risk acceptance: the risk is reasonably low and accepted without action, possibly with monitoring, because addressing all small risks is not feasible."},
  {"tag": "1.3", "question": "LONG ANSWER (18 points): Explain what project risk is, how risks are evaluated, the sources of risk in IT projects, what makes a risk acceptable, and how risks are managed, including the four approaches.", "answer": "Definition: project risk = uncertainties in events or conditions that can influence the project outcome negatively (threats) or positively (opportunities) (Cagliano et al., 2015). Opportunities are risks too because their impact on the constraints was not anticipated (e.g. resources released early); opportunities can even be manufactured within risk tolerance (finishing ahead of time, rewards for early release). PMBOK: ‘an uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, has a positive or negative effect on one or more project objectives’ (Larson & Gray, 2015). Evaluation: risk is uncertain, so it is evaluated by probability of occurrence and size of damage (or benefit); probability changes as knowledge grows, and risks cease to exist once they occur or can no longer occur. Sources: inadequate staffing and technological/logistical support, limited funding, immature practices, lack of stakeholder communication, dependencies on external participants; identifying them matters because things will go wrong (Elzamly & Hussin, 2016). Known risks that cannot be managed proactively need contingency plans; unknown risks need management reserves; finishing early leaves time for unknown threats. IT sources (Taherdoost & Keshavarzsaleh, 2018): technological (poor integration/interfacing, underestimated users, no scalability), organizational (no technology acquisition strategy, insufficient resourcing, underestimated staffing; projects requested by leadership without understanding IT, leaving IT reactive), user-dependent. Acceptability: risk attitude (risk appetite and tolerance), shaped by environmental and idiosyncratic factors; risk-averse (pessimistic), risk-neutral (reserved about utilities and payoff), risk-taking (optimistic) (Figure 6: utility vs payoff curves); risk threshold = affordable level of uncertainty or impact (below acceptable, above intolerable). Attitude is influenced by perception, tolerances, biases, drives, interests, cognitive capabilities and emotional state, which can cause deliberate ignorance (Kutsch & Hall, 2010). Management: four steps: identification (basis for handling; in planning and monitoring), analysis (probability, damage, measures), decision, monitoring (implemented adequately, stays acceptable; low risks can become serious, e.g. several developers ill). Four approaches: mitigation (reduce probability or damage to an acceptable level), acceptance (reasonably low risks, possibly monitored; too many small risks to address all), transfer (to an external entity, e.g. insurance or service provider), avoidance (risk too high vs benefit, e.g. not outsourcing a task with high security risks). The choice depends on risk attitude, cost of mitigation/transfer, and probability and potential damage."}

]
